Background and definitions.

An abstract polytope of rank n is a partially ordered set P with a strictly monotone rank function onto {-1, 0, 1, ..., n} such that:
(i) P has a unique minimum element F_{-1} (of rank -1) and a unique maximum element F_n (of rank n);
(ii) every maximal chain (flag) of P has length n+2, containing exactly one element of each rank;
(iii) P is strongly flag-connected: for any two flags Φ, Ψ, there is a sequence of flags Φ = Φ_0, Φ_1, ..., Φ_k = Ψ, each containing Φ ∩ Ψ, with consecutive flags differing in exactly one element;
(iv) the diamond condition holds: whenever F < G with rank(G) - rank(F) = 2, there are exactly two elements H with F < H < G.

A rank-3 abstract polytope is called a polyhedron.

An (abstract) map on a surface determines a rank-3 incidence structure with vertices, edges and faces. Equivalently, a map can be encoded by a pair of permutations (or a group action) on its flags ("darts"), giving rise to a poset of rank 3 with vertex-edge-face incidence. Such a flag poset always satisfies the diamond condition and has flags of the correct length, but it need not satisfy strong flag-connectedness, nor the requirement that any two i-faces share at most one (i-1)-face, nor other conditions necessary for it to be an abstract polytope (polyhedron).

A map M is called chiral if its automorphism group acts on the flags with exactly two orbits, such that adjacent flags lie in different orbits (so M admits "rotational" symmetry but no reflection symmetry).

Problem. Give an intrinsic characterization of those chiral maps M (in the combinatorial/topological sense above) whose associated rank-3 incidence poset is not an abstract polytope; equivalently, determine necessary and sufficient conditions on a chiral map for it to fail at least one of the defining axioms of an abstract polyhedron.

